includes all [downloadable content](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Downloadable_content "Downloadable content") (DLC) for the Wii U version of *Mario Kart 8*, including characters, courses, and vehicle components.[[15]](#cite_note-IGN2-19)[[6]](#cite_note-GI2-10) These features do not need to be unlocked.[[16]](#cite_note-SN-20) In addition to previous DLC, several new characters were introduced in *Deluxe*, including the [Inklings](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inkling_%28Splatoon%29 "Inkling (Splatoon)") from *[Splatoon](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Splatoon "Splatoon")*, [Bowser Jr.](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bowser_Jr. "Bowser Jr."), [Dry Bones](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dry_Bones_%28Mario%29 "Dry Bones (Mario)"), and [King Boo](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_Boo "King Boo").[[17]](#cite_note-NLife-21)[[18]](#cite_note-EGM-22) Characters from third-party franchises, including [Sonic the Hedgehog](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonic_the_Hedgehog_%28character%29 "Sonic the Hedgehog (character)"), [Mega Man](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mega_Man_%28character%29 "Mega Man (character)") and [Pac-Man](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pac-Man_%28character%29 "Pac-Man (character)"), are present as costumes for the Mii character unlocked by scanning [Amiibo](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amiibo "Amiibo"), in addition to those based on Nintendo characters.[[19]](#cite_note-23) Other changes include the ability to hold two items at once,[[20]](#cite_note-EuroG2-24)[[21]](#cite_note-GRadar2-25) and [accessibility](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accessibility "Accessibility") features to assist newer players, such as auto-acceleration or smart steering, which prevents players from veering off course.[[17]](#cite_note-NLife-21)[[6]](#cite_note-GI2-10) *Deluxe* also reintroduces the [Boo](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Mario_franchise_characters#Enemy_characters "List of Mario franchise characters") item, which allows the player to temporarily become invisible and steal an opponent's item, and in Battle, reintroduces the Feather, which gives the player a small jump boost, which allows you to jump over your opponents.[[22]](#cite_note-Destruct2-26)[[5]](#cite_note-HCG-9) In *Deluxe*'s "Booster Course Pass", several courses have differing layouts in every lap, condensing multiple variations of a course into one.[[23]](#cite_note-DestructoidWave1-27)

[![Two characters in split-screen view on a concrete surface. On the left is Bowser Jr., a yellow turtle-like creature with a spiked green shell who is riding in a small circular car. In front of him is a Piranha Plant, a venus flytrap-like creature with a white-spotted red head. On the right is Isabelle, a humanoid yellow dog on a motorbike. Isabelle is driving toward a blue switch with a key icon placed below a yellow and blue cage. In the center of the screen is a minimap displaying the characters' location in the course.](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/74/Renegade_Roundup.webp/250px-Renegade_Roundup.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARenegade_Roundup.webp)

In Renegade Roundup, the authority player [Bowser Jr.](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bowser_Jr. "Bowser Jr.") (left), using a [Piranha Plant](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piranha_Plant "Piranha Plant"), aims to capture [Isabelle](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabelle_%28Animal_Crossing%29 "Isabelle (Animal Crossing)") (right), who is trying to free her captured renegade teammates from a cage by driving over a key switch.

The largest addition to *Mario Kart 8 Deluxe* is the updated battle mode. *Deluxe* includes five new battle mode-exclusive courses and three battle courses adapted from previous entries in the *Mario Kart* series. These battle courses are designed specifically for the mode, featuring layouts that allow for easier item play, rather than repurposing standard race courses like *Mario Kart 8*.[[24]](#cite_note-GSpot2-28) The Feather item, reintroduced from previous entries in the series, is exclusive to this mode and allows the player to get a small boost in height when it is activated.[[21]](#cite_note-GRadar2-25)[[22]](#cite_note-Destruct2-26) There are five rulesets in battle mode.[[15]](#cite_note-IGN2-19) In Balloon Battle, players use items to destroy each other's balloons.[[15]](#cite_note-IGN2-19) Unlike in previous incarnations, the mode is point-based rather than [last-man-standing](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Last_man_standing_%28video_games%29 "Last man standing (video games)"), meaning that the winner is whoever destroys the most balloons before the match ends.[[24]](#cite_note-GSpot2-28)[[25]](#cite_note-GTM2-29)[[4]](#cite_note-USG2-8) Bob-omb Blast is a variation of Balloon Battle with all the items replaced with bombs.[[24]](#cite_note-GSpot2-28)[[26]](#cite_note-NLife-DX-30)[[6]](#cite_note-GI2-10) In Shine Thief, players must maintain control of the [Shine Sprite](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shine_Sprite "Shine Sprite") for as long as possible;[[20]](#cite_note-EuroG2-24) the player or team 

